rvel Studios it was its first modern  movie that in itself is incredibly  significant handling the script was  burgeoning screenwriter David s Goyer  who would go on to write Christopher  Nolan's critically lauded Dark Knight  trilogy new line brought Gore in to  adept blade into a campy vampire hunting  movie but the young writer had different  plans he wanted to take blade seriously  starring as blade was of course Wesley  Snipes there probably hasn't been a more  pitched perfect superhero casting ever  except for maybe Robert Downey jr. as  Iron Man  Snipes lent an absolutely undeniable  level of cool to the character he took  the character so seriously that you had  no choice but to as well  not to mention the fact that Snipes  himself is an incredible martial artist  who choreographed all of the film's  fight scenes Boyer and director Stephen  Norrington made the decision to age up  the film to appeal to adult comic fans  this wasn't a cute movie it hit hard it  was seedy grimy gory and most  importantly grounded in a way that no  superhero film had been before meanwhile  they efficiently established the inner  workings of blades world in which a  hidden Society of vampires pull all the  strings from behind the scenes it's a  rich world parallel to ours similar  enough to remind us of the world we  inhabit but full of otherworldly  concepts in which the audience could  lose itself it was this kind of world  building that would lend a significant  amount of relatability to films like The  Dark Knight and The Avengers that world  soon unveils an impressive roster of  incredible supporting characters from  blades grizzled mentor Whistler played  by Kris Kristofferson to Stephen Dorff  slide Deacon Frost a villainous vampire  looking to upend the ancient vampire  establishment and eliminate blade to dr.  Karen Jenson a courageous hematologist  portrayed by anna Boucher writes who  saves blades life multiple times  throughout the film and had so many  things going f